Here's a clear comparison of useEffect vs useLayoutEffect:

**useEffect** (Most common choice):
```typescript
// Runs after render, asynchronously
useEffect(() => {
  // Good for: API calls, subscriptions, non-visual updates
  const data = await fetchUserData();
  setUserData(data);
}, []);
```

**useLayoutEffect** (Visual updates):
```typescript
// Runs synchronously before browser paint
useLayoutEffect(() => {
  // Good for: DOM measurements, visual updates
  const elementWidth = ref.current.getBoundingClientRect().width;
  setTooltipPosition(elementWidth);
}, []);
```

Key differences:
- useEffect: Async, better performance, use by default
- useLayoutEffect: Sync, blocks painting, use only for visual glitches

Real example where useLayoutEffect matters:
```typescript
// Prevents tooltip flicker when measuring position
const Tooltip = ({ text }) => {
  const [pos, setPos] = useState({ x: 0, y: 0 });
  const tooltipRef = useRef<HTMLDivElement>(null);

  useLayoutEffect(() => {
    if (tooltipRef.current) {
      const rect = tooltipRef.current.getBoundingClientRect();
      setPos({ 
        x: rect.width / 2,
        y: rect.height
      });
    }
  }, [text]);

  return <div ref={tooltipRef}>{text}</div>;
};
```

